****
The Oxygen triplet in the infrared, at 777 nanometers, is a really strange one. A relatively strong signal shows that there is something, but it is very weak on details. However, what details, just what details.

In the row spectrum, much like with helium, it is in emission beyond the limb. 
There is a very obvious chromosphere, with the triplet protruding well beyond the limb. 
The light of prominences is sometimes blurry, perhaps due to some Doppler shifts going on, 
sometimes clearly shifted, and sometimes just straight.

On the disk, it is a different story. On the quiet disk, it is in obvious absorption, 
but a keen observer will see that it has, here and there on the continuous, somewhat blurry lines, some darker knots, or 
outright spectacular Doppler shifts with very obvious light deficit around flares.

The reconstructed disk will thus show a generally dull image with lost contrast compared to the continuum, 
somewhat darker plages, and somewhat less dark sunspots. Flares darken, sometimes considerably. 

With considerable processing, continuum removal etc., further details emerge. 
Beyond the limb, above active regions, there is a light blanket similar to Helium's. 
On the disk, the dark flares will show details, 
and filaments that appear strong, dense and dark in H&alpha;, may reveal themselves. 
That is right, filaments - Oxygen thus joining the club of elements H, He, Na, Mg, Ca where I've shown filaments.

Then there is the question of <em>smudges</em>. The oxygen disk may show obvious, long lasting darkened spots 
in places that are rather unassuming in most wavelengths. On this particular picture, on this particular day, there's none, 
the picture was chosen for the filaments.
In a previous article I've shown that a smudge is rather unassuming bright-ish plages in:
H&alpha;, Ca II 8542, Na I D; nothing obvious, small sunspot aside, in: continuum, Fe I 7781, and is dark in
(O I 7772, hence smudge), He I D3, He I 10830 and H Paschen gamma. From space, a 
smudge usually corresponds to something smoldering but not flaring in AIA/SUVI 304.
